Here's how to implement global error handling using ASP.NET Core's built-in middleware:

**1. Add Problem Details services and exception handler in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Register ProblemDetails service
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

var app = builder.Build();

// Use the exception handler middleware (global catch-all)
app.UseExceptionHandler();

// Use status code pages for non-exception error responses
app.UseStatusCodePages();
```

**2. Create a custom `IExceptionHandler` implementation:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class GlobalExceptionHandler : IExceptionHandler
{
    private readonly ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> _logger;

    public GlobalExceptionHandler(ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Resource not found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Invalid request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError, "An unexpected error occurred")
        };

        _logger.LogError(exception, "Unhandled exception: {Message}", exception.Message);

        var problemDetails = new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Type = $"https://httpstatuses.com/{statusCode}"
        };

        // Only include detail for non-500 errors to avoid leaking sensitive info
        if (statusCode != StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError)
        {
            problemDetails.Detail = exception.Message;
        }

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problemDetails, cancellationToken);

        return true; // Exception was handled
    }
}
```

**3. Register the handler in `Program.cs` (before `builder.Build()`):**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();
```

**How it works:**
- `UseExceptionHandler()` catches all unhandled exceptions globally
- Your `IExceptionHandler` maps exception types to appropriate HTTP status codes
- Responses automatically use the `application/problem+json` content type
- No sensitive stack traces or details leak on 500 errors
- No try-catch needed in individual endpoints

**Example response for a `KeyNotFoundException`:**

```json
{
  "type": "https://httpstatuses.com/404",
  "title": "Resource not found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "User with ID 42 was not found"
}
```

This requires .NET 8+. The `IExceptionHandler` interface was introduced in .NET 8 as the preferred way to handle exceptions in minimal APIs.
